.TH PERROR 3
.SH NAME
perror, sys_errlist, sys_nerr \- system error messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B void perror(const char *s);
.PP
.B extern int sys_nerr;
.br
.B extern char *sys_errlist[];
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Perror
produces a short error message
on the standard error file
describing the last error encountered during a call
to the system from a C program.
First the argument string
.I s
is printed, then a colon, then the message and a new-line.
Most usefully, the argument string is the name
of the program which incurred the error.
The error number is taken from the external variable
.I errno
(see
.IR intro (2)),
which is set when errors occur but not cleared when
non-erroneous calls are made.
.PP
To simplify variant formatting
of messages, the vector of message strings
.I sys_errlist
is provided;
.I errno
can be used as an index in this table to get the
message string without the newline.
.I Sys_nerr
is the number of messages provided for in the table;
it should be checked because new
error codes may be added to the system before
they are added to the table.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
intro(2)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/perror.c, errlst.c, include/errno.h
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The table is 36 entries, and no more is possible
The value the
.B $77
gate leaves in r14 is always one of the numbers <errno.h> defines: the kernel
assigns from its own copy of the list, and b6sim folds the host's numbering onto
the same set.  Nothing else can arrive from a system call, so a
.I sys_nerr
check can never fail on a kernel error \- which is not to say it should be
dropped.
.PP
.B The last three are not kernel codes at all.
33 and 34 \- EDOM and ERANGE \- are set by libm, and 35 \- EILSEQ \- belongs to
the multibyte conversions.  They are in the table because C11 sec 7.5 requires
all three to exist and
.I perror
must be able to name them.  Nothing sets EILSEQ yet.
.SS An array of char * is one word per element
A fat pointer fits in a word, marker bit and byte offset and all, so this table
costs 36 words and not 36 pointers' worth of anything larger.
.SS errno cannot be read at the call site in C
r14 is caller-saved and the compiler loads it with the negative argument count
before every call, so any C statement between the system call and the test would
have destroyed it.  Each stub tests r14 itself and branches to a shared
.I cerror,
which stores the value into
.I errno
and returns \-1.
.B That is the whole reason lib/libc/sys is assembly and not C,
and it is why a stub that forgets the branch still returns \-1 \- the gate puts
it there \- with only
.I errno
to give it away.
